 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE MAJOR OAK. The most famous tree in England, perhaps a thousand years old, propped up on stilts because it is very old and a bit tired. It is said to have sheltered Robin Hood and his band. The tree is not telling."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "ARCHERY GLADE. A clearing where visitors may try a longbow. The targets are straw. The signs say 'please aim at the targets'. The signs have been shot at, too, which is why this one is written in very small letters."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 45,
   "text": "HERNE'S CLEARING. The old stories say a hunter with antlers rides through the forest at dusk with hounds that no one quite sees. He's not cruel, only very fond of the chase. If you hear horns, say good evening."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "SHERWOOD FOREST. Once it covered a fifth of the county. Much is fields now, but an ancient core of oaks survives. The way through is marked; please stay on it. The outlaws left years ago, but the nettles stayed."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "ROBIN HOOD. Nobody agrees when he lived, or if. In the earliest ballads he lived in Barnsdale, in Yorkshire. Then Nottingham got the story, then Hollywood moved it all about. The forest has no view on the matter."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "LARDER OF THE OUTLAWS. Hunters in the old forest laws had to leave the king's deer alone. A good ballad made it a game. A bad winter made it a necessity. Please do not hunt the visitors."
  }
 ],
 "spawn": [
